{
  "id": "Compound::DB00220 - Gene::1555",
  "verbalization": "Nelfinavir, a protease inhibitor, is shown to bind to Cyp3a5 and Cyp3a4, which are members of the CYP3A family of enzymes. These enzymes are involved in the metabolism of a wide range of substrates, including steroids and other xenobiotics. The binding of Nelfinavir to Cyp3a5 and Cyp3a4 leads to their participation in steroid hydroxylase activity, which is a key pathway in the metabolism of steroid hormones. This activity is ultimately linked to Cyp2b6, suggesting that Nelfinavir may inhibit Cyp2b6 through the modulation of CYP3A enzymes. The evidence from the three paths indicates that Nelfinavir exerts its effect on Cyp2b6 via a mechanism involving the inhibition of CYP3A enzymes, which in turn affects the activity of Cyp2b6. The integration of these pathways highlights a potential role for Nelfinavir in modulating Cyp2b6 activity through the regulation of CYP3A enzymes, providing a plausible mechanism for its interaction with Cyp2b6.",
